{"name":"Alphabet Blue","hex":"#abcdef","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"alphabet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The set of letters used when writing in a language.","A writing system in which letters represent phonemes. \nA writing system in which there are letters for the consonant and vowel phonemes."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To designate by the letters of the alphabet; to arrange alphabetically."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/alphabet"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Alphabet","blocks":[{"text":"An alphabet is a writing system that uses a standard set of symbols called graphemes – more commonly known as letters – to more or less represent particular sounds in a spoken language. Specifically, these graphemes largely correspond to phonemes, which are the smallest sound segments that can distinguish one word from another in a given language. Not all writing systems represent language in this way: a syllabary assigns symbols to spoken syllables, while logographies assign symbols to words, morphemes, or other semantic units."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Phoenician_alphabet.svg/330px-Phoenician_alphabet.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alphabet"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Ba%60alat.png/250px-Ba%60alat.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ec/Ba%60alat.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Ba%60alat.png/250px-Ba%60alat.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A specimen of the Proto-Sinaitic script, one of the earliest phonemic scripts","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABa%60alat.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Old_Hungarian_alphabet_of_J%C3%A1nos_Telegdi.jpg/500px-Old_Hungarian_alphabet_of_J%C3%A1nos_Telegdi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Old_Hungarian_alphabet_of_J%C3%A1nos_Telegdi.jpg/1280px-Old_Hungarian_alphabet_of_J%C3%A1nos_Telegdi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Old_Hungarian_alphabet_of_J%C3%A1nos_Telegdi.jpg/500px-Old_Hungarian_alphabet_of_J%C3%A1nos_Telegdi.jpg?utm